{/* `themWord` is passed in rather than hard-coded in the script below: the hub
    renders for whichever of them holds the cookie, so a literal "he" in a success
    message is wrong half the time.

    ITS PROVENANCE CHANGED AND THE OLD NOTE HERE WOULD HAVE LIED ABOUT IT. This
    used to read "one of `she` / `he` — never user text", which was a closed set
    of two literals and therefore no injection surface at all. It is now a name
    out of US_HER_NAME, so it is OPERATOR text: still not attacker-reachable —
    nobody but Sam can set a Vercel environment variable, and it takes a redeploy
    — but no longer a two-value enum, and the honest version of the sentence is
    that Astro's define:vars JSON-serialises it rather than that it cannot vary. */}
{/* `vapidKey` is the PUBLIC half of the VAPID pair and nothing else from
    config.ts comes near this script. It is public by construction — the push
    service embeds it in the subscription it issues — so there is no secret being
    rendered here, and the private key is never read on this page at all. It is
    base64url from `web-push`'s own generator, so there is no character in it that
    could close a string and start a statement. Empty string when the feature is
    unconfigured, in which case the block above never rendered either. */}
<script is:inline define:vars={{ serverNowMs: nowMs, themWord, vapidKey }}>
/* --------------------------------------------------------------------------
   THREE JOBS, AND DELIBERATELY NO MORE.

   Rendered unconditionally rather than wrapped in a template expression: a
   <script> inside `{...}` asks the Astro compiler to read JavaScript braces as
   template braces, and the failure mode is a build error in a file that reads as
   correct. Each block below bails out on its own if its markup is absent.

   WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT: any script for the LIST. Add, tick and remove are
   native forms that 303 back, they lose nothing on failure, and every line of
   script that is not here is a line that cannot break the accessible path.
   -------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
(function () {
    /* ====================================================================
       1. THE CLOCKS TICK.

       WHY A TICK IS WARRANTED AT ALL: the server renders the true time, and it
       is true for about thirty seconds. This wing is an installed PWA whose
       start_url is this page, so "opened it this morning and left it open" is
       the normal case, not the edge — a server-only clock would sit there
       reading 08:14 all afternoon, which is worse than showing nothing.

       WHY IT IS ANCHORED TO THE SERVER'S CLOCK: `skew` is measured ONCE
       against the server's own `Date.now()` at render, and every subsequent
       frame renders `Date.now() - skew`. So a phone whose clock is four
       minutes fast still shows the correct time in both zones. Trusting the
       device clock outright would have quietly replaced a correct
       server-rendered value with a wrong client-rendered one.

       WHY IT IS NOT AN aria-live REGION: it would announce the time every
       minute, forever, which is actively hostile. The <time> text and its
       `datetime` are updated silently; a screen reader reads the current value
       whenever the user navigates to it, which is exactly the right behaviour.

       Intl does the zone arithmetic, so the device's own timezone is
       irrelevant — only that its clock is roughly right, which `skew` then
       corrects anyway.
       ==================================================================== */
    var clockEls = document.querySelectorAll('[data-tg-clock]');
    if (clockEls.length) {
        var skew = Date.now() - serverNowMs;

        var pad = function (n) { return n < 10 ? '0' + n : String(n); };

        var paintClocks = function () {
            var at = new Date(Date.now() - skew);
            for (var i = 0; i < clockEls.length; i += 1) {
                var el = clockEls[i];
                var tz = el.getAttribute('data-tz');
                if (!tz) continue;
                try {
                    var parts = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-CA', {
                        timeZone: tz,
                        year: 'numeric', month: '2-digit', day: '2-digit',
                        hour: '2-digit', minute: '2-digit',
                        hourCycle: 'h23'
                    }).formatToParts(at);
                    var g = function (type) {
                        for (var k = 0; k < parts.length; k += 1) {
                            if (parts[k].type === type) return parts[k].value;
                        }
                        return '';
                    };
                    var hh = pad(Number(g('hour')) % 24);
                    var mm = g('minute');
                    /* textContent, never innerHTML. Nothing here is user text, but
                       the rule is absolute in this wing and a future edit that
                       introduced one would otherwise be an XSS. */
                    el.textContent = hh + ':' + mm;
                    /* The machine-readable value has to move with the visible one,
                       or `datetime` slowly becomes a lie about the same element.
                       The offset is kept from the server-rendered attribute: it only
                       changes at a DST boundary, and re-deriving it here would mean
                       reimplementing tzOffsetMinutes in the browser. */
                    var prev = el.getAttribute('datetime') || '';
                    var offset = prev.slice(16);
                    el.setAttribute(
                        'datetime',
                        g('year') + '-' + g('month') + '-' + g('day') + 'T' + hh + ':' + mm + offset
                    );
                } catch (err) {
                    /* An unknown timezone throws in Intl. The server-rendered value
                       is already correct-at-render, so the honest failure is to stop
                       ticking and leave it alone rather than to blank it. */
                    return;
                }
            }
        };

        /* Aligned to the top of the next minute, then every minute. A naive
           60s interval started mid-minute is visibly wrong for up to 59
           seconds at a time. */
        var tick = function () {
            paintClocks();
            setTimeout(function () {
                paintClocks();
                setInterval(paintClocks, 60000);
            }, 60000 - (Date.now() % 60000));
        };
        tick();

        /* iOS freezes timers in a backgrounded tab, so an installed app brought
           back after lunch would paint a stale time until the next interval
           fires. Repaint on the way back in. */
        document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', function () {
            if (!document.hidden) paintClocks();
        });
    }

    /* ====================================================================
       2. THINKING OF YOU, WITHOUT A PAGE NAVIGATION.

       The form already works: 303, re-render, done. What a native submit
       cannot do is complete without navigating, and for a single tap on a long
       page that means losing her scroll position every time. That is the whole
       purchase — the endpoint, the copy and the outcome are identical.
       ==================================================================== */
    var thinkForm = document.getElementById('think-form');
    var thinkBtn = document.getElementById('think-send');
    var thinkSaid = document.getElementById('think-status');
    var thinkSent = document.getElementById('think-sent');

    if (thinkForm && thinkBtn && thinkSaid) {
        var THINK_MSG = {
            rate: 'give it a minute, then try again.',
            store: "that didn't send — not your fault. Try again?",
            unauthorized: 'your session ran out. Answer the questions again and you are back in.',
            unconfigured: 'something is unplugged on the server. Not your fault.',
            'cross-site': 'that did not come from this page, so it was refused.'
        };
        var thinkLabel = thinkBtn.textContent;

        thinkForm.addEventListener('submit', async function (event) {
            event.preventDefault();
            thinkBtn.disabled = true;
            thinkBtn.textContent = 'sending';

            var res, data;
            try {
                res = await fetch('/api/us/thinking', {
                    /* JSON in, JSON out. The endpoint keys its response shape off
                       this header, so the no-JS form POST still gets its 303. */
                    method: 'POST',
                    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
                    credentials: 'same-origin',
                    body: '{}'
                });
                data = await res.json();
            } catch (err) {
                thinkSaid.textContent = 'hm — the server did not answer. Nothing was sent.';
                return;
            } finally {
                thinkBtn.disabled = false;
                thinkBtn.textContent = thinkLabel;
            }

            if (!res.ok || !data || data.ok !== true) {
                var code = (data && data.code) || 'no';
                thinkSaid.textContent =
                    'hm — ' + (THINK_MSG[code] || 'that did not go through. Nothing was sent.');
                return;
            }

            /* `sent: false` is a SUCCESS — the coalesce, not an error. Both
               branches read as something warm rather than as a rejection. */
            thinkSaid.textContent = data.sent
                ? 'sent. ' + themWord.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + themWord.slice(1) + ' will see it.'
                : 'already sent — one is enough for now.';

            if (thinkSent) {
                /* `data.ago` is rendered SERVER-SIDE by warmAgo() and assigned
                   with textContent. Deliberately not composed here: a second
                   implementation in the browser would have to know both
                   timezones, the part-of-day bands and the wing calendar, and
                   would disagree with the server at exactly the boundaries
                   nobody tests. */
                thinkSent.textContent = 'you sent one ' + (data.ago || 'just now') + '.';
            }
        });
    }

    /* ====================================================================
       3. HER ANSWER, WITHOUT LOSING WHAT SHE TYPED.

       The only reason this block exists: a native submit that fails has
       already navigated away and taken up to four hundred characters with it.
       Everything it buys is that on failure her answer is still in the box.

       On SUCCESS it hands control back to the server with a reload rather than
       painting the revealed pair itself. Patching it here would mean a second
       renderer in JavaScript — paragraph splitting, the mirrored cards, the
       sealed-versus-revealed branch — that can disagree with the Astro one, and
       a card that disagrees with the store is worse than a reload. It is also
       the only way the REVEAL is guaranteed to come from the server, which is
       where the seal lives.
       ==================================================================== */
    var ansForm = document.getElementById('answer-form');
    var ansBtn = document.getElementById('answer-send');
    var ansOut = document.getElementById('answer-status');
    var ansBox = document.getElementById('answer-text');

    if (ansForm && ansBtn && ansOut && ansBox) {
        var ANS_MSG = {
            empty: 'there is nothing in the box yet.',
            'text-missing': 'there is nothing in the box yet.',
            'too-long': 'that is longer than the box will hold. Trim it a little?',
            frozen: 'you both already answered that one — it is set now, on purpose.',
            closed: 'that day has closed. Today’s is open.',
            'bad-date': 'that is not a real day.',
            'bad-request': "the server couldn't read that. Nothing was saved.",
            unauthorized: 'your session ran out. Answer the questions again and you are back in.',
            unconfigured: 'something is unplugged on the server. Not your fault.',
            store: "that didn't save. Nothing was lost — your answer is still here. Try again?",
            rate: 'give it a minute, then send it again.'
        };

        var say = function (kind, text) {
            /* The word is part of the message, so this never depends on colour. */
            /* UNHIDE BEFORE WRITING. A live region that appears with its
               text already inside it does not announce: the screen reader sees
               one mutation adding a whole subtree, not a change within a region
               it was already watching. So it must enter the a11y tree FIRST and
               gain its text SECOND. today.astro and dj.astro already did it this
               way; the hub, the letters page and the gate did not, so every
               outcome they reported was silent. */
            ansOut.dataset.kind = kind;
            ansOut.hidden = false;
            ansOut.textContent = (kind === 'bad' ? 'hm — ' : '') + text;
        };

        var ansLabel = ansBtn.textContent;

        ansForm.addEventListener('submit', async function (event) {
            event.preventDefault();

            if (!ansBox.value.trim()) {
                say('bad', 'write something first.');
                ansBox.focus();
                return;
            }

            ansBtn.disabled = true;
            ansBtn.textContent = 'saving';

            var res, data;
            try {
                res = await fetch('/api/us/together', {
                    method: 'POST',
                    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
                    credentials: 'same-origin',
                    body: JSON.stringify({
                        action: 'answer',
                        date: ansForm.elements.date ? ansForm.elements.date.value : '',
                        text: ansBox.value
                    })
                });
                data = await res.json();
            } catch (err) {
                say('bad', 'the server did not answer. Nothing was saved, and what you wrote is still here.');
                return;
            } finally {
                ansBtn.disabled = false;
                ansBtn.textContent = ansLabel;
            }

            if (!res.ok || !data || data.ok !== true) {
                var code = (data && data.code) || 'no';
                say('bad', ANS_MSG[code] || 'that did not go through. Nothing was saved.');
                return;
            }

            say('ok', data.revealed
                ? 'in — and you were both there. Loading it now.'
                : 'in. Nothing of theirs shows up until they answer too.');

            /* Straight back to the server. The pause is so the line above is
               readable first. */
            /* usLand rather than assign: `?ok=answered#question` is the same URL on a
               second answer, and an assign() there is a same-document navigation that
               re-fetches nothing — so editing an answer would have confirmed and then
               shown the old one. See public/us-land.js. */
            setTimeout(function () {
                var path = '/samdrea/vault';
                var search = '?ok=' + (data.revealed ? 'revealed' : 'answered');
                if (window.usLand) window.usLand(path, search, '#question');
                else location.assign(path + search + '#question');
            }, 700);
        });
    }

    /* ====================================================================
       4. NOTIFICATIONS.

       ---------------------------------------------------------------------
       IT NEVER ASKS BY ITSELF. NOT ONCE.

       `Notification.requestPermission()` is only ever reached from a real tap on
       the button. Calling it on load is refused outright by some platforms, and
       on the ones where it works it is a modal system dialog thrown at somebody
       who was reading — which is exactly the behaviour that trains people to
       press Don't Allow, and Don't Allow is PERMANENT. One ask, on her terms, is
       also the only ask that has a decent chance of being a yes.

       There is no reminder, no second prompt, no badge, and no state where this
       block asks twice. If she leaves it alone it stays alone.

       ---------------------------------------------------------------------
       FIVE STATES, ONE TRUE AT A TIME, ALL OF THEM HONEST

         nope       this browser cannot do it. iOS needs 16.4 or newer.
         install    iOS, and not running from the home screen. Push does NOT
                    work in a Safari tab, so a switch here would fail the moment
                    she used it. It says what to tap instead.
         blocked    permission denied. Terminal in the browser — requestPermission
                    resolves 'denied' forever without prompting — so there is no
                    button, and the copy names the system setting.
         ask        not asked yet, OR granted with no subscription (which is what
                    "she turned them off" looks like on the next page load).
         on         granted, and this device is registered.

       ---------------------------------------------------------------------
       WHY "GRANTED WITH NO SUBSCRIPTION" IS THE OFF SWITCH

       Turning them off cannot un-grant the permission — no API can. So if this
       read `permission === 'granted'` as "on", the next page load would re-register
       the device she had just turned off, and the off button would be a button that
       undoes itself. Asking the browser whether a SUBSCRIPTION exists is the real
       question, and it needs no localStorage and no server round trip to answer.

       ---------------------------------------------------------------------
       AND IT RE-REGISTERS SILENTLY WHEN IT IS ALREADY ON

       Not a nag — nothing is shown and nothing is asked. It is one idempotent POST
       that heals the one failure she could never diagnose: a subscription the
       browser still has and the store does not, which looks exactly like working
       notifications that never arrive. /api/us/push keys on the endpoint, so
       re-sending the same device is a no-op.
       ==================================================================== */
    var nBlock = document.getElementById('notify');
    if (nBlock && vapidKey) {
        var nSay = document.getElementById('notify-say');
        var nOnBtn = document.getElementById('notify-on-btn');
        var nOffBtn = document.getElementById('notify-off-btn');

        /* The five paragraphs. Exactly one is ever unhidden, so `state` hides all
           of them first — otherwise a transition would leave two true-sounding
           sentences stacked on top of each other. */
        var N_STATES = ['ask', 'on', 'blocked', 'install', 'nope'];

        var state = function (which) {
            for (var i = 0; i < N_STATES.length; i++) {
                var el = document.getElementById('notify-' + N_STATES[i]);
                if (el) el.hidden = N_STATES[i] !== which;
            }
            /* The buttons follow the state rather than being set alongside it, so
               there is no arrangement where "blocked" is showing over a button.

               The off button now lives INSIDE #notify-on, so the loop above already
               hides it with the row and this line is redundant. It is kept because
               the invariant it states — that button visibility is a function of
               state and nothing else — is the thing that was worth writing down, and
               a future move of either button out of its row should not silently
               reintroduce the bug. */
            if (nOnBtn) nOnBtn.hidden = which !== 'ask';
            if (nOffBtn) nOffBtn.hidden = which !== 'on';
            nBlock.hidden = false;
        };

        /* Same shape as the answer block's say(): unhide FIRST so the live region
           is already in the accessibility tree, then write, or the screen reader
           sees one subtree appear rather than a change inside a region it was
           watching, and says nothing. */
        var nsay = function (kind, text) {
            if (!nSay) return;
            nSay.dataset.kind = kind;
            nSay.hidden = false;
            nSay.textContent = (kind === 'bad' ? 'hm — ' : '') + text;
        };

        var nquiet = function () {
            if (nSay) { nSay.hidden = true; nSay.textContent = ''; }
        };

        /* base64url to the Uint8Array that applicationServerKey wants. The key is
           standard base64url with no padding, so the padding has to be put back
           before atob will look at it. */
        var keyBytes = function (b64) {
            var padded = (b64 + '===='.slice(0, (4 - (b64.length % 4)) % 4))
                .replace(/-/g, '+').replace(/_/g, '/');
            var raw = atob(padded);
            var out = new Uint8Array(raw.length);
            for (var i = 0; i < raw.length; i++) out[i] = raw.charCodeAt(i);
            return out;
        };

        /* THE SCOPE IS '/' AND IT HAS TO BE. The worker must be able to control
           /samdrea/* and a worker's scope cannot be broader than its own path, so
           it is served from the ROOT as /sw.js. It registers no fetch handler, so
           a root scope buys it nothing except the ability to be notified — see the
           comment at the top of public/sw.js, which is the whole argument. */
        var enroll = async function () {
            var reg = await navigator.serviceWorker.register('/sw.js', { scope: '/' });
            /* register() resolves before the worker is active, and pushManager on a
               registration with no active worker can reject. */
            await navigator.serviceWorker.ready;

            var sub = await reg.pushManager.getSubscription();
            if (!sub) {
                /* userVisibleOnly: true is REQUIRED — Chrome rejects the
                   subscription without it, and it is also the truth here: every
                   push this wing sends shows a notification. */
                sub = await reg.pushManager.subscribe({
                    userVisibleOnly: true,
                    applicationServerKey: keyBytes(vapidKey)
                });
            }

            var res = await fetch('/api/us/push', {
                method: 'POST',
                headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
                credentials: 'same-origin',
                body: JSON.stringify({ action: 'subscribe', subscription: sub.toJSON() })
            });
            var data = await res.json();
            if (!res.ok || !data || data.ok !== true) {
                throw new Error((data && data.code) || 'no');
            }
        };

        var N_MSG = {
            unauthorized: 'your session ran out. Answer the questions again and you are back in.',
            unconfigured: 'something is unplugged on the server. Not your fault.',
            'cross-site': 'that came from the wrong place, so nothing changed.',
            rate: 'give it a minute, then try again.',
            'bad-subscription': "your phone handed over something the server couldn't read."
        };

        /* hasOwnProperty, not a bare `N_MSG[code]`, because `code` here can be a
           DOMException message from subscribe() rather than one of our own codes.
           A bare lookup of an inherited key ('toString', 'constructor') returns a
           function, which is truthy, and she would be shown source code. */
        var nmsg = function (code) {
            return typeof code === 'string' && Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(N_MSG, code)
                ? N_MSG[code]
                : '';
        };

        /* ---- turn them on -------------------------------------------------
           The ONLY place requestPermission() is called, and it is inside a click
           handler so it is inside a user gesture. */
        if (nOnBtn) {
            nOnBtn.addEventListener('click', async function () {
                nquiet();
                nOnBtn.disabled = true;
                var label = nOnBtn.textContent;
                nOnBtn.textContent = 'just a second';

                try {
                    /* Skipped when it is already granted — which is the case after
                       she has turned them off and is turning them back on. Calling
                       it again would be harmless and would also be a second ask. */
                    var perm = Notification.permission;
                    if (perm !== 'granted') perm = await Notification.requestPermission();

                    if (perm === 'denied') {
                        state('blocked');
                        return;
                    }
                    if (perm !== 'granted') {
                        /* She closed the dialog without choosing. Nothing has been
                           decided, so it goes back to the way it was and says
                           nothing at all. */
                        state('ask');
                        return;
                    }

                    await enroll();
                    state('on');
                    nsay('ok', 'done. Your phone will let you know.');
                } catch (err) {
                    state('ask');
                    nsay('bad', nmsg(err && err.message) ||
                        "that didn't take. Nothing has changed, and you can try again.");
                } finally {
                    nOnBtn.disabled = false;
                    nOnBtn.textContent = label;
                }
            });
        }

        /* ---- turn them off ------------------------------------------------
           BOTH SIDES, and the local one is the one that matters. Dropping only the
           server's copy would leave the browser holding a live subscription that
           the silent re-register above would put straight back — an off switch
           that turns itself on again on the next page load. */
        if (nOffBtn) {
            nOffBtn.addEventListener('click', async function () {
                nquiet();
                nOffBtn.disabled = true;
                var label = nOffBtn.textContent;
                nOffBtn.textContent = 'just a second';

                try {
                    var reg = await navigator.serviceWorker.getRegistration('/');
                    var sub = reg ? await reg.pushManager.getSubscription() : null;

                    if (sub) {
                        /* The server FIRST, while the endpoint is still readable.
                           If this half fails the local half still happens, and the
                           store's copy is then a dead endpoint that gets pruned the
                           first time anything is pushed to it. */
                        try {
                            await fetch('/api/us/push', {
                                method: 'POST',
                                headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
                                credentials: 'same-origin',
                                body: JSON.stringify({ action: 'unsubscribe', endpoint: sub.endpoint })
                            });
                        } catch (err) { /* see above — the local unsubscribe carries it */ }

                        await sub.unsubscribe();
                    }

                    state('ask');
                    nsay('ok', 'off. Nothing will buzz.');
                } catch (err) {
                    nsay('bad', "that didn't take. They're still on — try again?");
                } finally {
                    nOffBtn.disabled = false;
                    nOffBtn.textContent = label;
                }
            });
        }

        /* ---- what is true right now --------------------------------------- */
        (async function () {
            /* Notification AND serviceWorker AND PushManager. All three, because
               iOS before 16.4 has the first two and not the third, and a browser
               with PushManager but no Notification cannot show anything. */
            var able = 'serviceWorker' in navigator &&
                'PushManager' in window &&
                'Notification' in window;
            if (!able) { state('nope'); return; }

            /* IS THIS THE INSTALLED APP? `navigator.standalone` is Safari's own
               answer and the only reliable one on iOS; display-mode covers
               everywhere else. iOS is detected by platform rather than by feature
               because the thing being detected IS a platform rule: push does not
               work in a Safari tab, full stop, and no feature test reports that
               until subscribe() has already failed. MacIntel with touch points is
               an iPad, which reports itself as a Mac. */
            var standalone = window.navigator.standalone === true ||
                (window.matchMedia && window.matchMedia('(display-mode: standalone)').matches);
            var isIOS = /iPad|iPhone|iPod/.test(navigator.userAgent) ||
                (navigator.platform === 'MacIntel' && navigator.maxTouchPoints > 1);
            if (isIOS && !standalone) { state('install'); return; }

            if (Notification.permission === 'denied') { state('blocked'); return; }
            if (Notification.permission !== 'granted') { state('ask'); return; }

            /* Granted. So the question is whether this device is actually
               subscribed, and the worker has to be registered to be asked. */
            try {
                var reg = await navigator.serviceWorker.register('/sw.js', { scope: '/' });
                await navigator.serviceWorker.ready;
                var sub = await reg.pushManager.getSubscription();

                if (!sub) {
                    /* Permission granted, no subscription. She turned them off, or
                       the browser dropped it. Either way it is OFF, and turning it
                       back on from here will not prompt her again. */
                    state('ask');
                    return;
                }

                /* Shown before the re-register, not after: it is already true, and
                   making her wait on a network round trip to be told so would put a
                   spinner on a settled state. */
                state('on');
                /* THE SILENT RE-REGISTER. Awaited so a failure is caught, and the
                   only thing a failure does is add one honest line. */
                await enroll();
            } catch (err) {
                /* The worker would not register, or the store would not take it.
                   Say so rather than showing "they're on" over notifications that
                   will never arrive. */
                state('ask');
                nsay('bad', "your phone is willing, but this didn't reach the server. Try the button?");
            }
        })();
    }
})();
</script>
</body>
</html>
